Q2.	How will you troubleshoot network issues in Linux.?

Answer: If there is a network issue, I would check
a. Connectivity Issue with the commands "ping" (ping <ip/host>)
b. DNS using the command "nslookup" (nslookup <domain name>) --> If ping 8.8.8.8 works but nslookup google.com fails, then its a DNS Problem
c. Network Interface is up using the command "ip link show" --> We can use "ifconfig" command also, but it is deprecated.
d. Routing using the command "ip route"
e. If the issue still persist, i will check for the firewall using the command "iptables"
f. If the above mentioned will not sort out the issue, I will go capture the packet using "tcpdump" and use "traceroute" to see where the packets stopped

Q3. LVM in Linux.?

Answer: LVM -> Logical Volume Manager.
It is a storage management system in linux, which gives flexibility to manage the storage.
Basically, disk partitions have a fixed size and are difficult to resize. Here, LVM creates logical volumes, which is easy to increase or decrease whenever we need more or less disk space.

Q4. How to run a process in background in Linux.?

Answer: To start the process in background directly, I use '&' at the end of the command. (command &)
To move the already running process to the background, I will do Ctrl + Z (to suspend the running process) and use "bg" command to resume the process in the background.
Check the background jobs using "jobs" command.
To bring the job back to foreground, i use "fg" command

Q5. How increase size of a file system in linux system.? Give command

Answer: There are two type of filesystems in Linux(ubuntu and debian) - a. ext4 (Fourth Extended FileSystem) and b. XFS
XFS is designed for large files and enterprise workloads. And it is default filesystem in RHEL and therefore in Openshift also we have XFS.

To determine which filesystem we are using we can run - "df -Th"
And to increase the size of this XFS filesystem first extend the logical volume "lvextend -L 10G /"we can use "xfs_growfs /mountpoint"

Incase of ext4 filesystem to increase that we need to increase the Logical volume first and then resize the fs with below commands - 
lvextend -L +10G /
resize2fs /

Note > why XFS is better than ext4 - XFS is designed for large files and enterprise workloads, Scales well, Excellent for DBs and mainly used in enterprise environments.

Q6. What is SELinux and its Policies.

Answer: SELinux stands for Security-Enhanced Linux. It's an additional security layer that controls what applications and users are allowed to access, even if standard Linux file permissions allow it. I usually explain it as a security guard inside a building—having a key card gets you into the building, but the guard still checks whether you're allowed into a specific room. SELinux has three modes: Enforcing, which blocks unauthorized actions; Permissive, which only logs violations; and Disabled, where SELinux is turned off. It uses policies, such as the Targeted policy, to define what each application is permitted to access, helping limit the impact of security incidents

